H1 estimates job completion horizon and favors the smallest estimated horizon. H9 emphasizes remaining work but sharply discounts slow machines and delayed completion, while the supplied selectors switch based on action-cost spread or variability; those costs measure immediate burden, not which H performs better. This selector uses action-cost contrast and progress to choose between those two distinct scheduling priorities.

```python
def use_heuristics(state):
    if state["availability"]["action_delta_best_gap"] and state["availability"]["action_delta_mean"] and state["availability"]["action_delta_std"]:
        gap = state["action_delta_best_gap"]
        mean = state["action_delta_mean"]
        std = state["action_delta_std"]
        if gap > 0.05 and std <= mean:
            if state["availability"]["construction_progress"]:
                if state["construction_progress"] < 0.4:
                    return 9
            return 1
        if gap > 0.01 and std > mean:
            return 9
    if state["availability"]["construction_progress"]:
        if state["construction_progress"] < 0.4:
            return 9
    return 1
